<ArticleLayout slug="subscription-or-managed-production" ctaLabel="Get a market range for your volume">
  <p class="standfirst">Every comparison of these two models argues about features, turnaround promises and monthly price. None of them starts with the question that decides the outcome: what does the fee actually buy? Answer that and the rest follows, including the part nobody publishes.</p>

  <h2>What is the difference between a design subscription and a production partner?</h2>

  <p>A subscription sells you access to creative capacity for a period. Production sells you named assets, delivered. That is the whole distinction, and it survives every feature table you will read, because it is the thing the feature tables are built to avoid.</p>

  <p>You can see it on the cards themselves. Subscription tiers price hours and concurrent lanes. Not one of them commits to a number of finished assets. So when two quotes sit side by side and one looks cheaper, you are usually comparing a price for capacity against a price for output, which is not a comparison at all.</p>

  <h2>Access or assets: what the fee is attached to</h2>

  <p>Read any card in this category closely and the priced unit turns out to be a period of availability. Sometimes that period is described in hours. Sometimes in how many jobs may be open at once. Never in deliverables.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">At the managed end of the category, the monthly fee buys a block of creative hours, published at 120 hours and 180 hours a month on one card read in September 2026, and unused hours do not roll over.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing page in this category, September 2026, read directly, single card, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Hours that expire. That is the honest shape of an access product, and it is not a criticism: gyms work the same way, and so do law firms. It just means the risk of not using the capacity sits with you, and the risk of the work taking longer than expected sits with you too.</p>

  <p>Production inverts both. The unit is a defined deliverable at a defined specification, and the risk of it taking longer than expected sits with whoever quoted it. That is why production quotes come with scope documents and subscriptions come with tier grids.</p>

