  <p>Note the last column. In the unlimited band there is no denominator at all: no hours, no asset counts, nothing you could divide the fee by. You cannot compute a unit cost even in principle. In the managed band you can compute a unit cost of access, and only of access.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Divide the published managed tiers by their published hours and the category prices access at almost exactly $50 a creative hour: $5,995 for 120 hours and $8,995 for 180 hours.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on one published rate card in this category, collected September 2026, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Two tiers on one card, four cents apart on an hourly basis. That is a rate card wearing a subscription's clothes, and knowing it changes the conversation you have on the call.</p>

  <p>One more thing worth saying about the numbers above: some of the category has stopped publishing them. At least one card read in September 2026 carries no price at all and routes the buyer to a call instead. So treat any band here as the visible part of the market, dated September 2026, not as its full shape.</p>

  <h2>Design subscription at volume: where the queue bites</h2>

  <p>The word "unlimited" refers to how many requests you may file. It has never referred to how many can be worked at once, and every card says so in the tier grid, usually one line below the price.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"Unlimited requests" is published alongside a cap on how many requests can be worked at once. Across four published cards read in September 2026, that cap runs from one to five active items at a time, rising with the tier.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, September 2026, tier labels read directly off the cards, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Four independent cards, all publishing the same mechanic in different words. Filing a request and starting a request are separate events, and the gap between them is where a campaign schedule quietly dies.</p>

  <p>Run it against a real shape of work. A launch needing forty finished assets inside two weeks does not fit into three lanes, and no amount of filing helps. The constraint is throughput, and throughput is lanes multiplied by how fast a lane clears, neither of which is the number on the pricing page.</p>

  <p>Turnaround promises do not rescue this either, because of what they are attached to.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Turnaround promises in this category are published against small or minor requests, not against whole jobs. Cards read in September 2026 publish next-day, same-day, 24-hour and 12-hour promises, each qualified by request size or tier.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, September 2026, read directly, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>A twelve-hour promise on a minor request is a real promise about resizes. It is not a promise about a brand film, and the cards are honest about that in a way the comparison articles ranking above them are not.</p>

  <h2>The mid-market gap, shown rather than asserted</h2>

  <p>Take a brand spending somewhere between $3,000 and $12,000 a month on production. That is a common enough shape: a growing consumer brand, one or two markets, real campaign work but no in-house studio. Here is what the published floors do to it.</p>

  <p>The highest published unlimited tier is about $4,500 a month. The lowest published managed tier is $5,995 a month. Between those two figures, a window roughly $1,500 wide, no card checked in September 2026 publishes a tier at all.</p>

  <p>Above that hole, the arithmetic gets worse rather than better. At $8,995 a month the brand buys 180 hours. Spread over about 22 working days that is roughly 8.2 hours a day, which is one full-time creative plus direction, in three concurrent lanes, with the unused remainder expiring monthly. It is a defensible purchase. It is also nowhere near a campaign capability.</p>

  <p>And the next step up is not a step.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The top published managed floor commits the buyer to $192,000 for the year: a $15,000 monthly minimum plus a $1,000 monthly software fee, on a twelve-month term.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: arithmetic on a published rate card in this category, collected September 2026, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>A brand budgeting $12,000 a month is $4,000 a month below that floor and cannot sign a twelve-month term at a number it does not have. So the published category offers it a product that cannot carry a campaign, or a product it cannot afford, and nothing between the two.</p>

  <p>That gap is the reason this page exists. Nobody is overcharging here. The category prices capacity in blocks, and blocks do not subdivide, so a buyer whose need falls between two blocks has nowhere to land.</p>

  <h2>What the fee does not cover</h2>

  <p>This is the part that most often surprises a buyer three weeks in, and it is published in plain sight.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">None of the published subscription cards read in this category in September 2026 lists photography, filming, talent booking or rights clearance as an included service. The published scopes cover design, illustration, motion graphics and video editing, with stock supplied under license rather than originated.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: four published pricing pages in this category, September 2026, each included-service list read directly and checked for capture, talent and clearance, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Read that carefully, because the honest version matters. It does not say subscriptions never involve capture. It says that across four cards read on one day, capture, talent and clearance appear on none of the included-service lists. If your work needs a photograph that does not exist yet, the fee you are comparing does not contain the making of it.</p>

  <p>Exclusions also run inside the tiers, not only across the models.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published tier grids in this category exclude named categories of work from the entry tier, including short-form video and motion, animation, long-form video editing, front-end development and multi-brand rollout support.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published tier comparison grid in this category, September 2026, read directly, single card, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>Short-form video excluded from an entry tier, in a year when short-form video is most of what brands actually need, is worth checking before signing rather than after.</p>

  <h2>When is a subscription genuinely the right buy?</h2>

  <p>Often. This page is not an argument against the model, and pretending otherwise would be easy to see through.</p>

  <p>A subscription fits when the work is a steady stream of small, well-specified jobs against assets that already exist. Resizes across formats. Sales decks. Social posts drawn from an existing library. Landing page variants. Work where the brief is short, the master asset is already made, and nobody needs to book a studio.</p>

  <p>It fits especially well when demand is flat rather than spiky, because you are renting a lane and a flat month uses the lane efficiently. It fits badly when demand arrives in campaign shapes, because campaigns are spikes by definition and a lane does not widen for them.</p>

  <p>The clearest signal is in your own brief history. If most of your requests begin with a file that already exists, the subscription model is aimed at you. If most of them begin with something that has to be photographed, filmed, cast or cleared, it is not, and the price comparison you are running is measuring the wrong thing.</p>

  <h2>The check to run before you sign either one</h2>

  <p>This takes about twenty minutes with a spreadsheet of last quarter's requests, and it settles the question better than any comparison article, this one included.</p>

  <p><strong>Count the origination share.</strong> Go through last quarter's briefs and mark each one as either starting from an existing asset or requiring something new to be made. The ratio is your answer. High existing-asset share points to subscription. High origination share points to production, and the subscription price is not comparable.</p>

  <p><strong>Find your worst concurrency week.</strong> Not your average month. Look for the week where the most separate jobs needed to be live at once, and count them. Compare that number to the published lane cap on the tier you are considering. If your worst week exceeds the lanes, the model will fail in exactly that week, which is the week that matters.</p>

  <p><strong>Compute a per-asset figure both ways.</strong> Take a real month. Divide the subscription fee by the assets you actually received that month, and do the same with a production quote for the same list. Neither vendor will give you this number, because neither prices in it. You have to build it yourself, and it is usually the first honest comparison anyone in the room has seen.</p>

  <p><strong>Read the exclusions before the inclusions.</strong> Tier grids sell on what is included. The line that will cost you is on the excluded side, and it is usually video, motion or rollout.</p>

  <p><strong>Price the expiry.</strong> If hours do not roll over, a quiet month is a full-price month. Multiply your quietest month by the fee and decide whether that is acceptable, because it will happen at least twice a year.</p>

