  <h2>How this page was built</h2>

  <p>Every band and mechanic above was read directly off a live published pricing page on 9 September 2026, one card at a time. No aggregator, no directory, no roundup, no vendor's summary of anybody else's prices. Bands built from two or more independent cards are given as ranges. Anything drawn from a single card says so in its source line.</p>

  <p>One absence is worth stating, because it shaped the page. Across four query phrasings, every page-one analysis of these two models is published by a company that sells one of them. There is no independent, method-stated study of the design subscription model to cite, which is why this page cites published prices and published terms instead of anybody's conclusions, including its own category's.</p>

  <p>Prices in this category change without notice, and at least one card has already moved to quote-only. The collection date sits on every figure for that reason. Reviewed September 2026.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about subscriptions and production</h2>

  <h3>What is the difference between a design subscription and a production partner?</h3>

  <p>A subscription prices access to creative capacity for a period, published as hours a month or as how many jobs may be open at once. Production prices named deliverables against a written specification. The practical consequence is who carries the overrun: with access, the buyer does, and with a delivered asset, the seller does.</p>

  <h3>What am I actually buying with a design subscription?</h3>

  <p>Lanes and time. Published cards in September 2026 price 120 to 180 hours a month at the managed end, and one to five concurrent active requests at the unlimited end. No card checked commits to a number of finished assets, so the fee attaches to availability rather than to output.</p>

  <h3>Do design subscriptions work at high volume?</h3>

  <p>They work at high volume of small jobs and fail at high concurrency. Published tiers cap active requests at one to five at a time, so a launch needing forty finished assets in two weeks does not fit regardless of how many requests you are allowed to file. Check your worst concurrency week, not your monthly average.</p>

  <h3>When is a subscription the right choice?</h3>

  <p>When most briefs start from an asset that already exists and demand is steady rather than spiky. Resizes, decks, social from an existing library and landing page variants all suit the model well. Work that needs photography, filming, casting or rights clearance does not, since none of the cards read in September 2026 lists those as included.</p>

  <h3>What do subscription price bands look like in 2026?</h3>

  <p>Two bands, an order of magnitude apart. Unlimited-request tiers publish roughly $700 to $4,500 a month, month to month. Managed tiers publish roughly $6,000 to $15,000 a month, with dedicated teams from about $30,000 on a twelve-month term. Between $4,500 and $5,995, no card checked publishes a tier at all.</p>
